#
# Loaded LAST of all the build.prop files
# (system -> system_ext -> system_dlkm -> vendor -> vendor_dlkm -> odm_dlkm ->
#  odm -> product, property_service.cpp:1176-1192), and a later file OVERRIDES
# an earlier one (LoadProperties assigns over an existing key and logs
# "Overriding previous property"). So anything put here beats vendor.prop.
#
# ⚠️ That is a real trap when copying from another device tree. onyx's
# product.prop sets bluetooth.profile.{bap.*,csip,mcp,ccp,hap,vcp}.enabled=false
# because onyx has no LE audio. Copying that list wholesale would silently
# override the `=true` values this device's vendor.prop sets and kill LE audio.
# Every value below is taken from THIS device's factory product/etc/build.prop,
# not from onyx.

# Bluetooth — classic profiles
#
# These have no built-in default: BluetoothProperties.sysprop declares them with
# no default value, and every service reads them as
# `BluetoothProperties.isProfileXxxEnabled().orElse(false)` (e.g.
# A2dpService.java, HeadsetService.java, HidHostService.java). Stock keeps them
# on the *product* partition, which PixelOS replaces wholesale — so with this
# file empty the device would come up with A2DP, AVRCP, HFP, HID host, OPP, PAN,
# PBAP and MAP all switched off. Only GATT would survive, because GattService is
# the one that uses `.orElse(true)`.
#
# The LE-audio profiles are deliberately NOT repeated here; they are set in
# vendor.prop and would only be at risk of being overridden from this file.
bluetooth.profile.a2dp.source.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.avrcp.controller.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.avrcp.target.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.bas.client.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.gatt.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.hfp.ag.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.hid.host.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.map.server.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.opp.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.pan.nap.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.pan.panu.enabled=true
bluetooth.profile.pbap.server.enabled=true

# Stock explicitly disables the HID *device* role (acting as a keyboard or mouse
# for another host). Keep parity rather than letting it default on.
bluetooth.profile.hid.device.enabled=false

# LE Audio is supported but must not be auto-connected, matching stock.
# PhonePolicy.java:102 reads this with a default of **true**, so leaving it unset is
# not the same as leaving it alone: we would auto-connect LE Audio where stock
# deliberately does not. Measured on the running stock ROM:
#   [ro.bluetooth.leaudio.le_audio_connection_by_default]: [false]
# The BAP/VCP/CSIP/MCP/CCP/HAP profiles stay enabled (see vendor.prop) — this only
# governs whether a newly bonded device is put on LE Audio without being asked.
ro.bluetooth.leaudio.le_audio_connection_by_default=false

# SIG vendor ID reported in the Device ID record. Verbatim from stock.
bluetooth.device_id.vendor_id=0x001D

# Storage
# FuseDaemon.cpp:2707 reads this with a default of false; stock enables it.
# Passthrough lets reads and writes on /sdcard bypass the FUSE round trip for
# files the daemon has already vetted, which is a straight I/O win with no
# behavioural change.
persist.sys.fuse.passthrough.enable=true

# Audio
# AudioFlinger's per-client shared-memory heap. Stock raises it from the AOSP
# default; keeping parity avoids client-side allocation failures with the buffer
# sizes the QTI audio HAL hands out.
ro.af.client_heap_size_kbyte=7168

# ── Remote Key Provisioning ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ★ Missing until session 17, and its absence is not subtle: this device is
# **RKP-only**. Stock says so itself (`remote_provisioning.tee.rkp_only=true` in
# product/etc/build.prop:67) — the TEE KeyMint instance ships with no factory
# attestation keys and is supposed to fetch them from Google at runtime.
#
# With `remote_provisioning.hostname` unset, RKP is switched off at the root:
# Settings.getDefaultUrl() (RemoteKeyProvisioning/.../utils/Settings.java:239-243)
# returns "" for an empty hostname, and RemoteProvisioningService.java:64-67 then
# answers every getRegistration() with
#   "RKP is disabled. System configured with no default URL."
# Measured on the running build: ~20 keystore2 lines a minute, forever, and
# `keymint_generate_key` failing — i.e. **no hardware key attestation at all**,
# which is what Play Integrity's DEVICE verdict and every banking app want.
#
# ⚠️ This also revises session 16's conclusion. `work/s16/attest/` found a failed
# QWES attestation-certificate licence in the persist partition and concluded
# "attestation is factory-broken, not ours to fix". The licence finding stands,
# but the conclusion does not follow: on an rkp_only device that certificate is
# not the attestation path in the first place — RKP is. We had simply never
# turned RKP on.
#
# Values are stock's, verbatim, not onyx's: onyx sets
# `remote_provisioning.strongbox.rkp_only` because it has a StrongBox instance.
# This device does not — `pm list features` reports only
# `android.hardware.hardware_keystore=300` with no `android.hardware.strongbox_keystore`
# — so the flag has to name the TEE instance instead.
#
# ── ⚠️ Session 20: RKP is switched OFF, and the paragraphs above are the
#    reasoning that led to switching it on. They are kept because they are still
#    correct about the mechanism; what they could not know is that it does not
#    work on this device. ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Turning RKP on did not produce attestation. It produced a failure loop.
# Measured on the running build, every boot, eleven times:
#
#     E KeymasterUtils:  IKMHal_sendCmd failed with rsp_header->status: -18
#     E android_keymaster: TA call to generate_csr_v2() failed with error: -18
#     E android.hardware.security.keymint-service-qti: MAC tag mismatch
#
# The TA refuses to produce a CSR. rkpd completes a network round trip to
# Google, gets a challenge, and then fails locally — so the failure is inside
# TrustZone, which this port does not replace and cannot change. The KeyMint HAL
# and every library under it are stock blobs, and these properties were
# byte-identical to stock's, so there was nothing on our side left to vary.
#
# The consequence the previous two rounds missed by counting lines instead of
# following them: 41 attestation-key requests per boot all failed with
# OUT_OF_KEYS_TRANSIENT_ERROR. Hardware key attestation does not work on this
# device. Plain TEE key generation is fine — keystore_cli_v2 generate
# --seclevel=tee succeeds — it is specifically attestation that is dead.
#
# ── ★★ TWO CORRECTIONS, both made by flashing and measuring ────────────────
#
# **1. `remote_provisioning.enable_rkpd` DOES NOTHING. It has ZERO readers.**
#
# A tree-wide search for the string finds exactly one hit, and it is an
# unrelated aconfig flag name in ConfigInfrastructure's WritableFlags.java:545.
# No AOSP code reads this property. Session 17 set it to `true` believing it
# armed RKP; session 20 set it to `false` believing that disarmed RKP. Both were
# inert, and the flashed build proved it: with enable_rkpd=false, rkpd still ran,
# still contacted remoteprovisioning.googleapis.com, and still failed the CSR.
#
# The actual switch is `remote_provisioning.hostname`:
#     RkpRegistrationCheck.java:114   if (SystemProperties.get(...).isEmpty())
#     Settings.java:239-243           getDefaultUrl() returns "" for an empty host
# and RemoteProvisioningService then answers every getRegistration() with
# "RKP is disabled. System configured with no default URL."
#
# So the line is GONE. It is stock parity with no reader — the same shape as the
# ro.vendor.dolby.dax.version line vendor.prop removed for the same reason.
# (⚠️ Kept as a comment rather than silently deleted because it is set on stock,
# so the next person WILL see it there and wonder why we do not.)
#
# **2. `rkp_only=false` worked, and the acceptance criterion I wrote was wrong.**
#
# The criterion was "grep -c OUT_OF_KEYS; 0 means attestation now works". It
# measured one failure mode. After flashing, OUT_OF_KEYS is indeed 0 — keystore2
# now logs a WARNING and falls back instead of hard-failing, which is exactly
# what rkp_only=false is for. But the fallback fails too, one layer down:
#
#     E keystore2: security_level.rs:680
#           1: Error::Km(r#SECURE_HW_COMMUNICATION_FAILED)
#        0: security_level.rs:674: While generating without a provided
#           attestation key and params: [... ATTESTATION_CHALLENGE ...]
#
# 44 times per boot, all from com.google.android.gms. So stock's rkp_only=true
# was HONEST: there is no usable factory attestation key either, and the TA
# cannot attest at all in this state. Attestation is unreachable on this device
# by either route, and no property setting changes that.
#
# rkp_only stays false anyway: with rkpd off it makes keystore2 fail fast on the
# factory path instead of waiting on a provisioning service that is not coming.
#
# ★ THE REAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERION, replacing the one that was too narrow:
#     adb logcat -d | grep -c SECURE_HW_COMMUNICATION_FAILED
#   That is the attestation failure itself. It is expected to stay non-zero
#   until somebody solves the TA problem; 0 would mean attestation started
#   working and this whole block should be revisited.
#     adb logcat -d | grep -c Rkpd
#   THIS is what these two lines buy: it should now be ~0, where it was ~500.
#   No rkpd, no network round trip, no CSR failure, on every boot.
#
# ⚠️ Do NOT test either with a runtime setprop — the requests happen while apps
# start during boot, so the values have to be in place before sys.boot_completed.
#
# ⚠️ remote_provisioning.hostname is deliberately NOT set. That is the off
# switch. An earlier draft of this comment said to leave it set "because
# removing it would put the tree back in the state session 17 diagnosed" — but
# session 17's premise was that RKP could work here, and it cannot.
remote_provisioning.tee.rkp_only=false
